<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sivadol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1734">1734</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Shivadol</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sivadol is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivasagar">Sivasagar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, built in 1734 by Ambika, queen of the Ahom king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siva-singha">Siva Singha</a>, on the bank of the great Sivasagar tank. Its tall central spire is one of the landmarks of the town, and it stands in one compound with the smaller Vishnudol and Devidol temples, a group typical of eighteenth-century Ahom temple building.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sivadol.jpg" alt="Sivadol" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aniruddha Buragohain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siva_Dol.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivasagar">Sivasagar</a>, formerly Sibsagar, was the capital of the Ahom kingdom until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> had migrated from South China in 1228 and set up their first capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charaideo">Charaideo</a>, 28 kilometres from Sivasagar, in 1253; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> came to prevail among them. The temples were built by Bar Raja Ambika, chief consort of Swargadeo Siva Singha (1714–1744), on the east bank of the Sivasagar tank, also called the Borpukhuri tank. The tank was made by raising embankments, so that its water stands above the surrounding ground; it covers 257 acres with a water spread of 129 acres and is 27 feet deep. A battle against the Moamoria rebels was fought near it in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaurinath-singha">Gaurinath Singha</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Sivadol is built of stone and brick in the Shikhara style of Ahom temple architecture. Its tower rises 104 feet (32 m) from a base 195 feet (59 m) round, with parallel ridges and furrows, and is crowned by a four-tiered mastaka of 8 feet and a golden kalasha. Four smaller towers, the angashikaras, flank its lower part. In the sanctum the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> linga is set in reverse. An antechamber with a do-chala roof, like a typical Assamese hut, joins the sanctum to an outdoor hall.</p><p>The outer walls carry bas-relief sculptures and floral designs, among them images of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a> with two, four, six, ten and sixteen arms. The sixteen-armed Durga, a pan-Himalayan theme, is shown riding her lion and spearing the buffalo-headed demon Mahishasura, reflecting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaktism">Shaktism</a> the Ahom royalty adopted. The Vishnudol has a tower with a honeycomb design and a criss-cross framework, and the Devidol, or Joidol, a tower of undulating outline; there is also a temple of Ram and Hanuman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Every year a large fair is held at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple during Mahashivaratri, and pilgrims come from all parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to offer puja. The Dol Yatra and Rath Yatra are held at the Vishnudol, and Durga Puja at the Devidol in September and October. A Buddhist shrine, a tower for watching wintering birds, the red-brick Tai Museum, a church and two mosques also stand on the banks of the tank.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sivasagar Sivadol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ahom kings patronised temples and tanks around Sivasagar, including the <b>Sivadol</b>, and the moidams at Charaideo were inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2024.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
